.TH LAZYTYPST 1 "" "lazytypst 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lazytypst \- browse, edit, and compile Typst documents in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lazytypst
[\fIFOLDER\fR | \fIFILE\fR.typ[:\fILINE\fR[:\fICOLUMN\fR]]]
.br
.B lazytypst
[\-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version | \-\-doctor]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lazytypst
lists the .typ files in a folder and opens them in an editor with a live preview.
It runs the
.BR typst (1)
command to compile. Without \fIFOLDER\fR, it uses the current folder. The folder is also the Typst project root.
With a .typ file, it opens the file at once. The folder of the file is the project root.
With \fI:LINE\fR or \fI:LINE:COLUMN\fR after the file name, the cursor starts there, for example main.typ:42:7.
.PP
The editor saves the file 300 ms after the last key and compiles it. The preview shows one page as an image.
The terminal needs an image protocol (kitty, sixel, or iTerm2) for a sharp page. Without one, the program draws half blocks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage and the keys.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version of lazytypst and the version of typst.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Check typst, the terminal image protocol, and the temporary folder. The exit code is 0 if all checks pass, and 1 if not.
.SH KEYS
.SS Keys in the file list
.TP
.B ? or F1
Show this help window.
.TP
.B F9
The mouse on or off. On: a click selects a file, a second click opens it, and the wheel moves the selection. The terminal cannot select by itself then: hold Shift and drag.
.TP
.B j or Down
Select the next file.
.TP
.B k or Up
Select the previous file.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the selected file.
.TP
.B n
Make a new .typ file. Type a path such as chapters/two. The ending .typ is added.
.TP
.B E
Export the PDF of the selected file next to it.
.TP
.B e
Edit the selected file in $VISUAL or $EDITOR, then open it here.
.TP
.B s
Switch the order: by path, or the newest change first.
.TP
.B y
Copy the absolute path of the selected file to the system clipboard.
.TP
.B /
Filter the list. Type a part of a path. Enter keeps the filter, Esc removes it.
.TP
.B r
Read the folder again, to show new files and to drop deleted files.
.TP
.B g or Home
Select the first file.
.TP
.B G or End
Select the last file.
.TP
.B m
Mark the selected file as the main file, or remove the mark. lazytypst then compiles the main file, whatever file you edit.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SS Keys in the editor
.TP
.B F1
Show this help window.
.TP
.B F2
Switch to another file. Type to filter, Enter opens. F2 again opens the file before this one.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-S
Save the file.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-B
Save and compile the file.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-E
Save the file and export a PDF next to it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-F
Search. Type the text, then Enter or Ctrl\-F for the next match. Alt\-R switches to a regular expression. Esc closes.
.TP
.B F11
Show the preview on the full screen, and back. There, the plus key and the minus key zoom, 0 shows the whole page, and the arrow keys move the view. Typing does nothing there.
.TP
.B F5
Turn the live compile off or on. Autosave stays on.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-G
Go to the first error of the last compile.
.TP
.B Alt\-Enter, Ctrl\-]
Open the file that the string under the cursor names, for example in #include "chapters/two.typ". The text is saved first. F2 F2 goes back.
.TP
.B F3
The preview follows the cursor, or not. When the cursor enters another section, the preview goes to the page of its heading. Alt\-Up and Alt\-Down still turn pages.
.TP
.B F6
Show the history: the saved versions of this file, with their age and what they would change. Enter restores one as an edit, and Ctrl\-Z undoes it. The versions are in the state folder, not in the project.
.TP
.B F7
Spell check on or off. It needs hunspell and a dictionary. The language comes from #set text(lang: "de"). Unknown words are underlined.
.TP
.B Alt\-;
Go to the next misspelled word and show what to do: a suggestion, add the word to your dictionary, or ignore it.
.TP
.B F9
The mouse on or off. On: a click puts the cursor there, a drag selects, the wheel scrolls the text, and the wheel over the preview turns pages. The terminal cannot select by itself then: hold Shift and drag.
.TP
.B F10
Change the layout: editor and preview side by side, editor above the preview, or editor only. A window under 100 columns starts stacked. The choice is kept.
.TP
.B F4
Show the outline: the headings of the file. Type to filter, Enter jumps to the heading, F4 or Esc closes.
.TP
.B Alt\-S
Replace the text of the last search. Type the new text. Enter replaces one match and goes to the next. Alt\-A replaces all. Esc stops. Press Alt\-S in the search prompt, too.
.TP
.B Alt\-G
Go to a line. Type the line number, or line:column, then Enter. Esc closes.
.TP
.B F8, Shift\-F8
Go to the next error, or the previous error. The search wraps at the ends.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-O
Open the last exported PDF in the system viewer (xdg\-open).
.TP
.B Alt\-Down
Show the next page.
.TP
.B Alt\-Up
Show the previous page.
.TP
.B Alt\-Home
Show the first page.
.TP
.B Alt\-End
Show the last page.
.TP
.B Esc
Save the file and go back to the file list.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Q
Save the file and quit.
.SS Editing keys in the editor
.TP
.B Ctrl\-U, Ctrl\-Z
Undo.
.TP
.B Alt\-A
Select all the text.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Redo.
.TP
.B Shift with arrow keys
Select text.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Copy the selection. It also goes to the system clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-X
Cut the selection. It also goes to the system clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Y
Paste the text that the text area holds.
.TP
.B Shift\-Tab
Remove one indent level from the line.
.TP
.B Enter
Break the line and keep its indent. After ( [ or { the new line has one level more.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-W
Delete the word before the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-K
Delete to the end of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-J
Delete to the start of the line.
.TP
.B Alt\-F, Alt\-B
Move one word forward or back.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-A, End
Move to the start or the end of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Home, Ctrl\-End
Move to the start or the end of the file. With Shift, select to there.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-V, Alt\-V
Scroll one page down or up.
.TP
.B Alt\-<, Alt\->
Move to the first or the last line. The column stays.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LAZYTYPST_TYPST
The program that runs Typst. The default is \fBtypst\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, the program uses no colors for text and borders.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
The editor for the key e in the file list. VISUAL wins over EDITOR.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
The folder for the state files. The default is ~/.local/state.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
The folder for the private page folder. The default is the temporary directory.
.TP
.BR TYPST_FONT_PATHS ", " TYPST_IGNORE_SYSTEM_FONTS ", " TYPST_PACKAGE_PATH ", " TYPST_PACKAGE_CACHE_PATH
Passed to typst.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/lazytypst/main-files
The main file of each project.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/lazytypst/last-files
The last file and its page for each project.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lazytypst-<process id>
The private folder for the preview pages. If XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is not set or not a folder of the user, it is $TMPDIR/lazytypst-<process id>. The program deletes it at exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR typst (1),
.UR https://github.com/Orpheus-21/lazytypst
the lazytypst project
.UE
